   The fastest is +0.22 C per window on thermal_zone18, over 3 interval(s) between fix windows. This is
   the drift the pairing removes from the reading, and it is the number that says the pairing is doing
   work rather than decorating the answer: it is measured over the same minutes as the reading.

   THE DESIGN'S BALANCE: the fix windows sat at mean position 4.50 in the run and the pinned
   windows at 4.50, so the imbalance is 0.00 window(s). Every difference above is taken between a
   fix window and a pinned window whose ORDER ALTERNATES, which is what keeps that number small: with
   a FIXED order inside the pair it would be 1.00 window, and every difference would carry one window
   of the drift.
   At the fastest drift measured above (0.22 C per window), 0.00 window(s) of imbalance is worth
   about 0.00 C of the reading.

== the verdict (the alternating design)
   the primary zone was PRE-REGISTERED, in the first window of the run and before any 'performance'
   write: thermal_zone12 (tsens_tz_sensor11). Nothing below chose it from the data -- and that matters, because the largest of
   the 21 means above would sit about two standard errors above zero with no effect at all. That is the
   shape docs 172 printed as a price.
   the mean of 4 pair difference(s) on it:  2.65 C   (standard error 0.10 C)
   so the two-standard-error interval is [2.45, 2.85] C, and this experiment calls 0.2 C a cost.
   the control, over the same minutes: the phone moved up to 0.22 C per window on its own (on thermal_zone18), and the pairing removes that.

   -> COST-MEASURED: pinning all four cores is worth 2.65 C on thermal_zone12 (tsens_tz_sensor11), and the whole two-standard-error
      interval [2.45, 2.85] is above the 0.2 C this experiment can resolve. At this load, and with the
      phone's own drift cancelled by the pairing rather than assumed absent, that is what the second
      heat cause costs in temperature.

   Read it as a READING and not as the governor fix's price in general: ambient is not controlled, the
   battery's charging state is not controlled, and the load is whatever the phone was doing -- which is
   why the busy figure is printed for every window above. What this design DOES control is the phone's
   own drift, two ways: the two halves of a pair are 30s apart instead of a run apart,
   and they sat at mean positions 4.50 and 4.50 in the run, an imbalance of 0.00 window(s) -- the alternating order is what keeps those together, and the balance line above is the check.
   What it cannot control is LAG: 30s may be short against the SoC's thermal time constant (still
   unmeasured), so this design can UNDER-report the cost. A longer window trades that bias for more
   drift inside a pair, and the control above is how the trade is checked.
   The zones are the instrument's numbers, normalised by it; this script does not divide.
   This run used: --pairs 4 --seconds 30 --settle 5.
   The cores read 'interactive' cpu0=interactive cpu1=interactive cpu2=interactive cpu3=interactive -- the state it started in.
   WALL CLOCK: this attempt took 370s: 330s in 8 window(s), 0s in
   the holds -- and this design has NONE (--pairs): the 8 window(s) above are the whole run, 41s each
   against the 30s asked for. Every bound above is in THESE seconds.

   --keep: this run's scratch directory is /tmp/zl1-governor-temp-ab.CLHLis (deltas, deltas.note, win.A/B/C, back).

