SECTION 4 -- WHAT THE FOUR RUNS DID NOT ESTABLISH
====================================================================================================

(a) THE HEAT IS NOT FIXED. These are four readings of one cause at an uncontrolled load, ambient
    temperature and charge state. The busy figure is printed for every window inside every run, and
    it is not the same in the two halves (fix windows about 1.21-1.28 cores of 4, pinned about
    0.74-0.76) -- that is a property of this phone under `performance` and it is printed rather than
    corrected for. The control line says the phone was moving 0.11-0.22 C per window on its own
    during each run; the pairing removes that, and the balance line says the two halves sat at the
    same mean position (4.50 and 4.50, imbalance 0.00 -- which is the alternating order doing work).

(b) LAG IS STILL UNMEASURED. The SoC's thermal time constant is unknown, so if it is comparable to
    the 30 s window the design UNDERSTATES the effect. Nothing here bounds that.

(c) THE PRIMARY ZONE CHANGED HANDS AGAIN. It was zone12 on runs 1 and 3 and zone9 on runs 2 and 4,
    because the pre-registered zone is the hottest sensor of window A.1 and that changes between
    runs. What is new is that the reading no longer depends on which one was picked: in run 1 all 21
    tsens zones read +0.75 to +2.65 C (every one positive), so the spread across zones is now the
    shape of the effect rather than noise around it.

(d) 5 s IS THE SETTLE AFTER EACH WRITE. Whether that is long enough for the write to take effect,
    and whether two adjacent windows of the same state stack, is not measured here. The only
    evidence that the alternating ORDER is load-bearing is offline (docs 175's m16).

(e) THE WINDOW MEAN IS NOT A CONTINUOUS RECORD. One sample a second replaces one sample per window,
    and the printed span line (up to 9-10 C on a single zone inside a 10 s window at near-idle) says
    the second-to-second noise is real. Taking the mean does not remove that noise, it removes the
    systematic part of the choice of instant.

====================================================================================================
SECTION 5 -- WHAT WAS NOT DONE
====================================================================================================

Nothing was flashed. No partition was written. /dev/qbt1000 was never opened. cnss was never
unbound. No service was restarted and the container was never stopped. The only writes this stage
made to the phone were `interactive`/`performance` into the four cores' scaling_governor, which is
what the instrument does by design, each written back to `interactive` by an EXIT trap and re-read;
all four cores read `interactive` after every run and at the end of the series. boot_id before and
after the series: 2fbf9f8e-deea-4955-ad0c-6bddf0fb14f5, unchanged.

====================================================================================================
SECTION 6 -- THE LAUNCHER, VERBATIM
====================================================================================================

#!/bin/sh
# Four paired runs with the docs-177 window mean, back to back, detached so a dropped ssh does not kill
# the series. Each run restores `interactive` on all four cores in its own EXIT trap.
echo "SERIES START :: uptime $(cut -d\" \" -f1 /proc/uptime) :: boot_id $(cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id)"
echo "thermal instrument md5: $(md5sum /tmp/zl1-thermal.sh)"
echo "governor instrument md5: $(md5sum /tmp/zl1-governor-temp-ab.sh)"
for n in 1 2 3 4; do
  echo "=== RUN $n START :: uptime $(cut -d\" \" -f1 /proc/uptime)"
  sh /tmp/zl1-governor-temp-ab.sh --yes --keep --pairs 4 > /tmp/gov-mean-run$n.log 2>&1
  echo "=== RUN $n END rc=$? :: uptime $(cut -d\" \" -f1 /proc/uptime)"
  sleep 30
done
echo "SERIES END :: uptime $(cut -d\" \" -f1 /proc/uptime) :: boot_id $(cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id)"

(the `cut: '"': No such file or directory` lines in its output are its own uptime stamps: a
double-quote escape survived two shells badly. They are harmless -- the value printed is the second
field of /proc/uptime either way -- and they are left in rather than edited out of the record.)

